box":{"b":546.2343343098959,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":65.33333333333333,"r":537.3333333333334,"t":557.2343343098959},"charspan":[0,142],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Abbreviations: ACL, anterior cruciate ligament; MCL, medial collateral ligament; PCL, posterior cruciate ligament; PLC, posterolateral corner."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Multiple graft types and fixation devices are used. Ipsilat eral knee autografts; including bone-patellar tendon-bone and hamstring tendons, are commonly used for reconstruction grafts in young patients. Allografts are usually reserved for older patients and those with multiple ligament injuries.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":465.9010009765625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":64.33333333333333,"r":303.6666666666667,"t":527.2343343098958},"charspan":[0,297],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"Multiple graft types and fixation devices are used. Ipsilat eral knee autografts; including bone-patellar tendon-bone and hamstring tendons, are commonly used for reconstruction grafts in young patients. Allografts are usually reserved for older patients and those with multiple ligament injuries."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Fixation devices can be grouped based on the location and device type. Interference screws may be used with bone plugs or soft tissue grafts and may be metallic or a radiolu cent composite material (~Fig. 9). Alternatively, the graft may be docked in a tunnel with extracortical fixation (i.e, a suture button) (~Fig. 10). Another fixation option includes surgical  staples Or screw-washer devices adjacent to the nonarticular tunnel aperture (~Fig. 9) Suture anchors may also be utilized for a soft tissue repair or ligament reconstruction ( ~Fig. 9). Awareness of these fixation options is important for evaluation of postoperative imaging char acteristics and potential complications such as loss of fixa tion with interference screws or inadequate deployment of extracortical buttons.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":299.5676676432292,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":64.33333333333333,"r":304.3333333333333,"t":466.5676676432292},"charspan":[0,788],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Fixation devices can be grouped based on the location and device type. Interference screws may be used with bone plugs or soft tissue grafts and may be metallic or a radiolu cent composite material (~Fig. 9). Alternatively, the graft may be docked in a tunnel with extracortical fixation (i.e, a suture button) (~Fig. 10). Another fixation option includes surgical  staples Or screw-washer devices adjacent to the nonarticular tunnel aperture (~Fig. 9) Suture anchors may also be utilized for a soft tissue repair or ligament reconstruction ( ~Fig. 9). Awareness of these fixation options is important for evaluation of postoperative imaging char acteristics and potential complications such as loss of fixa tion with interference screws or inadequate deployment of extracortical buttons."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Senior Author's Preferred Surgical Techniques","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":273.23433430989587,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":64.33333333333333,"r":272.0,"t":287.9010009765625},"charspan":[0,45],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Senior Author's Preferred Surgical Techniques"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(~Table 3) Full surgical technique details are available in the specifically referenced articles (e.g. tunnel size; knee fixation angle, order of graft fixation) Importantly; the native anatomy guides the desired tunnel locations; and a thorough understanding of the native anatomy is important for review of postoperative imaging:","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":453.5676676432292,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":314.0,"r":553.6666666666666,"t":525.9010009765625},"charspan":[0,331],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"(~Table 3) Full surgical technique details are available in the specifically referenced articles (e.g. tunnel size; knee fixation angle, order of graft fixation) Importantly; the native anatomy guides the desired tunnel locations; and a thorough understanding of the native anatomy is important for review of postoperative imaging:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Anterior Cruciate Ligament Reconstruction","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":429.9010009765625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":315.0,"r":497.0,"t":442.2343343098958},"charspan":[0,41],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"Anterior Cruciate Ligament Reconstruction"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"ACL is performed   with patellar tendon autograft:. Anatomical femoral and tibial tunnelsI0 are created, and the graft is generally secured with titanium interference screws in the femur and tibia (~Fig. 9) The femoral tunnel should be positioned distal to the Blumensaat line on lateral radiographs,28 and the tibial tunnel should be adjacent to the anterior root attachment   of the lateral meniscus on axial MRI sections (~Fig. 4). Staples may be utilized to secure the bone plug in a trough in cases of tunnel mismatch; occasionally one prong of the staple may skive along the tibial shaft; which has no implications for the clinical outcome_ graft -","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":287.56766764322913,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":314.0,"r":554.0,"t":431.2343343098958},"charspan":[0,654],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"ACL is performed   with patellar tendon autograft:. Anatomical femoral and tibial tunnelsI0 are created, and the graft is generally secured with titanium interference screws in the femur and tibia (~Fig. 9) The femoral tunnel should be positioned distal to the Blumensaat line on lateral radiographs,28 and the tibial tunnel should be adjacent to the anterior root attachment   of the lateral meniscus on axial MRI sections (~Fig. 4). Staples may be utilized to secure the bone plug in a trough in cases of tunnel mismatch; occasionally one prong of the staple may skive along the tibial shaft; which has no implications for the clinical outcome_ graft -"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The intent of this section is not to serve as a technical manual for surgical treatment but rather to describe surgical indications ( ~Table 2) as well as aspects of commonly used techniques   and potential   postoperative imaging findings key","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":225.23433430989587,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":63.666666666666664,"r":304.0,"t":274.23433430989587},"charspan":[0,243],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"The intent of this section is not to serve as a technical manual for surgical treatment but rather to describe surgical indications ( ~Table 2) as well as aspects of commonly used techniques   and potential   postoperative imaging findings key"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Posterior Cruciate Ligament Reconstruction","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":261.9010009765625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":314.3333333333333,"r":500.3333333333333,"t":273.9010009765625},"charspan":[0,42],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":"Posterior Cruciate Ligament Reconstruction"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"An arthroscopic-assisted double-bundle PCL reconstruction recreates the anterolateral bundle (ALB) with an Achilles tendon allograft and the posteromedial bundle (PMB) with a","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":225.9010009765625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":314.3333333333333,"r":553.3333333333334,"t":262.9010009765625},"charspan":[0,174],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"An arthroscopic-assisted double-bundle PCL reconstruction recreates the anterolateral bundle (ALB) with an Achilles tendon allograft and the posteromedial bundle (PMB) with a"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table 3 Postoperative radiographic and magnetic resonance imaging pearls","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":194.56766764322913,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":64.33333333333333,"r":359.3333333333333,"t":207.23433430989587},"charspan":[0,72],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/13","text":"Table 3 Postoperative radiographic and magnetic resonance imaging pearls"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Abbreviations: MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; PCL, posterior cruciate ligament:.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":50.23433430989587,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":64.0,"r":347.0,"t":62.56766764322913},"charspan":[0,82],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/14","text":"Abbreviations: MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; PCL, posterior cruciate ligament:."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"Seminars in Musculoskeletal Radiology Vol. 21 No. 2/2017","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":22.56766764322913,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":65.0,"r":236.0,"t":32.9010009765625},"charspan":[0,56],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/15","text":"Seminars in Musculoskeletal Radiology Vol. 21 No. 2/2017"}],"version":"1.10.0"}